# Prod auth snippet — imported by config/Caddyfile when ANTHIVE_AUTH_SNIPPET
# points at this file (done by ant-supervisor when ANTHIVE_PROD=1).
#
# On prod, nginx is the SSL terminator AND the Authelia gate (via
# auth_request).  By the time a request reaches Caddy on 127.0.0.1:8500
# it is already authenticated and nginx has stamped Remote-User /
# Remote-Groups / Remote-Email / Remote-Name onto the request.  Caddy
# trusts those headers and does NOT call Authelia itself.
#
# Why not still call forward_auth here?
#   - Double-gating doubles latency on every request.
#   - The browser's session cookie is set against the public hostname.
#     Caddy on the loopback port would see that public Host, but
#     cookies/redirects scoped at the public URL
#     are already what nginx handled — re-running forward_auth here is
#     redundant.
#
# No gate here: the @bypass_auth matcher in Caddyfile still
# ensures /.well-known/*, /register, /streamlit* are never blocked, but
# the actual gate is upstream in nginx.  This file MUST exist (Caddy
# errors out on a missing import target).
#
# IMPORTANT: 127.0.0.1:8500 MUST NOT be exposed to the outside world.
# Caddy is bound localhost-only by ANTHIVE_CADDY_BIND=127.0.0.1 in prod
# precisely because there's no auth at this layer.
#
# The upstream proxy MUST overwrite or drop client-sent Remote-User /
# Remote-Groups / Remote-Email / Remote-Name on every path (the nginx
# examples in config/examples/ do). A public vhost without a login must
# drop them, or a visitor can claim `Remote-Groups: admins`.
#
# The legacy X-Remote-* names are no identity anywhere (ant-serve ignores
# them); drop whatever the upstream lets through.
request_header -X-Remote-User
request_header -X-Remote-Groups
